# Backend config for running Spring Boot directly on the host — e.g. from
# an IDE, or `./mvnw spring-boot:run` / `java -jar target/app.jar` — outside
# Docker/docker-compose. Not read by `docker compose up`: for the compose
# stack, docker-compose.yml passes the equivalent values into the `backend`
# container's environment straight from the repo-root `.env` (see
# `.env.example` there), and this file is never loaded in that path.
#
# Values below intentionally mirror the repo-root `.env.example` (Postgres
# 16, database `trendly`, user `trendly`, same dev-only JWT placeholder) so
# a host-run backend talks to the same local Postgres a `docker compose up`
# would provision. The one required difference is the datasource host:
# inside the compose network the DB is reachable at `postgres:5432` (the
# service name); from the host it's `localhost:5432` (or whatever you've
# overridden POSTGRES_PORT to in the root `.env`) because Postgres also
# publishes that port to the host in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Usage: cp backend/.env.example backend/.env (gitignored), then either
# export these into your shell/IDE run configuration, or use a plugin like
# `spring-boot-dotenv` / your IDE's built-in .env support — Spring Boot does
# not load `.env` files itself. Requires `postgres` to actually be running
# and reachable at the host/port below (e.g. `docker compose up postgres`
# from the repo root, or a local Postgres 16 instance of your own).

# --- Datasource (bound via Spring's relaxed env-var binding: BE-1.1) ---
# Port below assumes the default POSTGRES_PORT (5432) from the root
# .env.example / docker-compose.yml. If you overrode POSTGRES_PORT there
# (e.g. due to a local port conflict), change the port here to match.
SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/trendly
SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME=trendly
SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=trendly_dev_password

# --- Auth ---
# Dev-only placeholder. Never use this value outside local development.
# Matches the root .env.example's JWT_SECRET so tokens issued by a
# host-run backend and a compose-run backend stay interchangeable in dev.
JWT_SECRET=dev-only-change-me-please-do-not-use-in-prod

# --- JVM options (optional) ---
# JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS is a standard JDK-native env var, honored by any `java`
# launch (including `./mvnw spring-boot:run` / `java -jar` / most IDE run
# configs) -- not Docker-specific. Uncomment and set to tune JVM flags for
# a host-run backend, e.g.:
# JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS=-Xmx512m
#
# JAVA_OPTS (also optional) is only meaningful inside the Docker image
# (backend/Dockerfile's entrypoint shell-expands it into the `java`
# command); it has no effect when running the backend directly on the
# host, so it's not set here -- see docker-compose.yml / docs/local-dev.md
# for its Docker-only usage.
